Subject: Machine Checks Explained (was: CIA Machine Check - crashes)
In-reply-to: Your message of Mon, 01 Dec 1997 19:00:54 -0500.
	<Pine.LNX.3.95.971201183827.12387B-100000@mail.sjcme.edu> 
Mime-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain
Date: Mon, 01 Dec 1997 22:02:00 +0500

>>> "Benjamin J. Greslick" said:
> 
> Vector= 0X660 la_ptr=OXFFFFFC.OOOOOO5AO

Machine checks are of four (4) types, as in the following table:

Code      Reason                  Example or Common Cause
====      ======                  =======================

620	System Correctable        correctable errors in the memory subsystem

630	Processor Correctable     correctable cache and TLB errors

660	System Uncorrectable      unrecoverable memory errors

670	Processor Uncorrectable   unrecoverable cache or TLB errors, or
                                  read of a non-existent I/O space location

Since yours was of type 660, I'd suspect a SIMM with bad location(s).

Try reseating the SIMMs after cleaning the contacts; if this doesn't help,
try using only half the memory, or swapping out SIMMs until you locate the
culprit.

Good luck.

--Jay++

