From denk@cica.es Tue Jan 20 19:03:46 1998
Message-ID: <34C4D995.41C67EA6@iis.fhg.de>
Date: Tue, 20 Jan 1998 21:39:06 +0100
From: Claus Denk <denk@cica.es>
Subject: Re: Gaussian or other on Alpha/Linux?

Robert Williams wrote:
> 
> It is also necessary to "make" Gaussian
> on your DEC Unix machine using the public
> domain linear algebra General Matrix Multiply
> subroutine.  DEC's DXML library is very clever.
> It looks (I think) at the kernel of the machine it
> is to be loaded to for license information, and
> won't run under Linux.  DEC's GEMM is
> about four times as fast as the public domain
> version.  Surprizingly, the performance is still
> quite good.  A profile of the system shows that
> GEMM is important but not so much as to
> make it uncompetitive with other alternatives
> at the same price.  Brute force wins here.

You can get a very fast version of GEMM for free:

ftp://ftp.enseeiht.fr/pub/numerique/BLAS/RISC/

This SGEMM performs at 400 Mflpos, DGEMM at 290 Mflops on my
500 MHZ 1 Mb Cache when compiled with the DU f77 compiler (the
netlib version does 144 and 115 MFlops respectively). This 
should get quite close to the DXML library version. 
When compiling, be shure to set the cache size correctly for
the 21164 (it is preset to the 21064 cache size). I am currently
working on a version that should perform well also when compiled
with g77.

regards, Claus

